f=A.c5(A.a5(["Python","# Linear search to find a target value\ndef linear_search(arr, target):\n    for i in range(len(arr)):\n        if arr[i] == target:\n            return i  # Target found, return its index\n            \n    return -1  # Loop finished, target not found\n","Java","// Linear search returning the index\nint linearSearch(int[] arr, int target) {\n    for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {\n        if (arr[i] == target) {\n            return i; // Target found\n        }\n    }\n    return -1; // Target not found\n}\n","C++","#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint linearSearch(int arr[], int size, int target) {\n    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {\n        if (arr[i] == target) {\n            return i;\n        }\n    }\n    return -1;\n}\n","JavaScript","// Linear search function\nfunction linearSearch(arr, target) {\n    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {\n        if (arr[i] === target) {\n            return i;\n        }\n    }\n    return -1;\n}\n"],d,d),q,e,"Searching is the process of finding the location of a specific element within an array. Linear Search is the most straightforward method: it checks every single box from left to right until it finds the target. While simple, it becomes slow as the array grows larger.","array-searching",m,"Array Searching",A.a(["Returning 'not found' too early inside the loop instead of waiting for the loop to finish","Using Linear Search on a massive, sorted array instead of Binary Search","Checking out of bounds if using a while loop instead of a standard for loop"],h),f,A.a([A.Z(1,"Linear Search checks elements one by one in a straight line (sequentially) until it finds a match or reaches the end of the array.",A.a(["It repeatedly divides the array in half.","It checks each element sequentially from the first index to the last.","It sorts the array first, then looks for the element.","It uses a mathematical formula to calculate the index."],h),l),A.Z(3,"The best-case scenario is O(1). This happens when the element you are looking for is incredibly lucky and happens to be the very first item in the array!",A.a(["O(n)","O(log n)","O(n\xb2)","O(1)"],h),k),A.Z(1,"The worst-case scenario is O(n). This occurs when the target element is at the very end of the array, or isn't in the array at all, forcing the computer to check every single item.",A.a(["O(1)","O(n)","O(log n)","O(n\xb2)"],h),j),A.Z(1,"If an array is small and unsorted, Linear Search is perfect because it requires no complex sorting or setup overhead. For massive, sorted arrays, Binary Search is a much better choice.",A.a(["When searching through a massive, sorted database.","When the array is unsorted and relatively small.","When you need to search a 3D array.",i],h),"When is Linear Search usually the best algorithm to choose?")],c),"assets/mds/dsa/array/array_searching.md",B.acH)
