q=A.c5(A.a5(["Python",'# Delete element at a given index by shifting left\ndef delete_at(arr, size, index):\n    if index < 0 or index >= size:\n        return "Invalid index!"\n    deleted = arr[index]\n    # Shift all elements after index one step left\n    for i in range(index, size - 1):\n        arr[i] = arr[i + 1]\n    arr[size - 1] = 0   # Clear the duplicate last slot\n    return size - 1, deleted   # new size and deleted value\n',"Java","// Delete at index and return new size\nint deleteAt(int[] arr, int size, int index) {\n    int deleted = arr[index];\n    for (int i = index; i < size - 1; i++) {\n        arr[i] = arr[i + 1];  // shift left\n    }\n    arr[size - 1] = 0;  // optional cleanup\n    return size - 1;\n}\n","C++",'#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    int arr[] = {12, 22, 45, 74, 36};\n    int size = 5, index = 2;\n    for (int i = index; i < size - 1; i++) {\n        arr[i] = arr[i + 1];\n    }\n    arr[size - 1] = 0;\n    size--;\n    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) cout << arr[i] << " ";\n    return 0;\n}\n',"JavaScript","// Delete at index in a fixed-size simulation\nfunction deleteAt(arr, size, index) {\n    const deleted = arr[index];\n    for (let i = index; i < size - 1; i++) {\n        arr[i] = arr[i + 1];   // shift left\n    }\n    arr[size - 1] = 0;  // clear stale last slot\n    return { newSize: size - 1, deleted };\n}\n"],d,d),o,p,"Deletion removes an element from a specific position and closes the gap by shifting subsequent elements left. Removing the last element is O(1), but any other deletion is O(n) due to the required shift. The critical rule: always shift from the deletion point forward, never from the beginning.","array-deletion",m,"Array Deletion",A.a(["Forgetting to update the size counter \u2014 the array still 'holds' the stale value at the last slot","Shifting in the wrong direction (right instead of left) overwrites valid data","Not validating the index \u2014 deleting at index -1 or beyond size causes corruption"],h),q,A.a([A.Z(0,"To maintain contiguous memory, you must take every element to the right of the deleted index and shift it one step to the left to overwrite the gap.",A.a(["By shifting all elements after the gap one position to the left.","By shifting all elements before the gap one position to the right.","By swapping the deleted element with the last element.","By leaving the gap empty (null)."],h),"When deleting an element from the middle of an array, how do you close the gap?"),A.Z(1,"Deleting the first element (index 0) is the worst-case scenario. Every single remaining element in the array must be shifted one step to the left, taking O(n) time.",A.a(["O(1)","O(n)","O(log n)","O(n\xb2)"],h),"What is the time complexity of deleting the very first element of an array?"),A.Z(1,"Shifting simply copies values. The last slot will still hold the old, stale data. This is why we must decrement the 'size' counter so the program knows to ignore that duplicate slot.",A.a(["It is automatically deleted by the compiler.","It holds a duplicate of the element that was just shifted to its left.","It throws an out-of-bounds error.","It becomes a negative number."],h),"In standard array deletion, what happens to the very last slot after all elements have been shifted left?"),A.Z(2,"Lazy deletion avoids the expensive O(n) shifting process by simply replacing the value with a 'tombstone' marker (like null or -1) to tell search algorithms to ignore it.",A.a(["Waiting for the garbage collector to delete the array.","Deleting elements only when the computer has idle CPU time.","Marking a slot as 'deleted' using a special value (like -1) instead of shifting elements.","Shifting elements but not reducing the size counter."],h),"What is 'Lazy Deletion' (or Soft Deletion) in the context of arrays?")],c),"assets/mds/dsa/array/array_deletion.md",B.Hv)
