j=A.c5(A.a5(["Python",'# O(1) - Constant Time Complexity\ndef get_first_item(arr):\n    return arr[0] if arr else None\n\n# O(n) - Linear Time Complexity\ndef print_all_items(arr):\n    for item in arr:\n        print(item)\n\n# O(n\xb2) - Quadratic Time Complexity\ndef print_all_pairs(arr):\n    for first in arr:\n        for second in arr:\n            print(f"{first}, {second}")\n',"Java",'// O(1) - Constant Time Complexity\nint getFirstItem(int[] arr) {\n    return arr.length > 0 ? arr[0] : -1;\n}\n\n// O(n) - Linear Time Complexity\nvoid printAllItems(int[] arr) {\n    for (int item : arr) {\n        System.out.println(item);\n    }\n}\n\n// O(n\xb2) - Quadratic Time Complexity\nvoid printAllPairs(int[] arr) {\n    for (int first : arr) {\n        for (int second : arr) {\n            System.out.println(first + ", " + second);\n        }\n    }\n}\n',"C++",'// O(1) - Constant Time Complexity\nint getFirstItem(const vector<int>& arr) {\n    return !arr.empty() ? arr[0] : -1;\n}\n\n// O(n) - Linear Time Complexity\nvoid printAllItems(const vector<int>& arr) {\n    for (int item : arr) {\n        cout << item << "\\n";\n    }\n}\n\n// O(n\xb2) - Quadratic Time Complexity\nvoid printAllPairs(const vector<int>& arr) {\n    for (int first : arr) {\n        for (int second : arr) {\n            cout << first << ", " << second << "\\n";\n        }\n    }\n}\n',"JavaScript","// O(1) - Constant Time Complexity\nfunction getFirstItem(arr) {\n    return arr.length > 0 ? arr[0] : null;\n}\n\n// O(n) - Linear Time Complexity\nfunction printAllItems(arr) {\n    arr.forEach(item => console.log(item));\n}\n\n// O(n\xb2) - Quadratic Time Complexity\nfunction printAllPairs(arr) {\n    for (let first of arr) {\n        for (let second of arr) {\n            console.log(`${first}, ${second}`);\n        }\n    }\n}\n"],h,h),q,i,"Decode the ultimate metric of algorithmic survival! Master Big O Notation, the mathematical framework used by software engineers globally to predict code scalability. Learn how to peel back constants, simplify algebraic algorithms, and evaluate runtime behaviors across the structural spectrum.","algorithms-big-o-notation",m,"Big O Notation",A.a(["The Constant Trap: Thinking O(1) is always faster than O(n). If an O(1) operation takes 5 hours to complete once, a linear swap loop might be faster for small values of n.","Dropping the Wrong Terms: Forgetting to drop non-dominant terms (e.g., mistakenly identifying O(n\xb2 + n) as O(n\xb2 + n) instead of simplify-reducing it cleanly to O(n\xb2)).","Loop Counting Assumptions: Assuming nested loops always equal O(n\xb2). If the inner loop runs a fixed number of constants instead of scaling with n, it remains O(n)."],l),j,A.a([A.Z(1,"Big O notation tracks upper-bound growth rates. It models how performance requirements structurally increase as the input scope size (n) scales toward infinity, regardless of system speeds.",A.a(["The exact number of megabytes consumed by an active software module.","How the execution resource requirement of an algorithm scales relative to an increasing input size (n).","The compiler compilation throughput benchmarks across multi-threaded modern CPUs.","The baseline runtime variation between localized cloud servers."],l),"What is Big O notation fundamentally designed to measure?"),A.Z(1,"As n reaches extreme volumes, constants like +5 or coefficients like times 2 become insignificant next to the pure scale of n. Big O prioritizes the foundational growth trajectory.",A.a(["Because integers cause runtime arithmetic evaluation crashes.","Because as the input size (n) becomes infinitely large, constants and smaller terms provide negligible impact on the overall growth trend curve.","Because modern compilers automatically remove constants during code optimization passes.","Because constants are only used to calculate physical space complexity profiles."],l),"When analyzing algorithmic complexity, why do we drop constants and non-dominant terms (e.g., simplifying O(2n + 5) into O(n))?"),A.Z(2,"Two sequential, independent loops result in O(n) + O(n) = O(2n). Dropping the constant coefficient leaves us with a simplified growth trend of O(n).",A.a(["O(n\xb2)","O(2n)","O(n)","O(1)"],l),"An algorithm performs a linear search loop over an array, followed by a completely separate, independent linear loop. What is the final simplified Big O rating?"),A.Z(3,"Quadratic Time Complexity O(n\xb2) scales terribly because its execution work squares with the input. If the input size increases 10x, execution steps explode by 100x.",A.a(["O(1)","O(n log n)","O(n)","O(n\xb2)"],l),"Which of the following complexity trends represents the WORST scaling performance as the dataset grows massive?")],g),"assets/mds/dsa/algorithms/bigonotation.md",B.HF)
