a=A.c5(A.a5(["Python",'class Node:\n    def __init__(self, val):\n        self.val = val\n        self.left = None\n        self.right = None\n\ndef in_order(node):\n    if node is None:\n        return\n        \n    # 1. Traverse Left FIRST\n    in_order(node.left)\n    \n    # 2. Visit Root MIDDLE\n    print(node.val, end=" ")\n    \n    # 3. Traverse Right LAST\n    in_order(node.right)\n',"Java",'class Node {\n    int val;\n    Node left, right;\n    public Node(int item) { val = item; }\n}\n\nclass TreeTraversal {\n    public void inOrder(Node node) {\n        if (node == null) return;\n        \n        // 1. Traverse Left FIRST\n        inOrder(node.left);\n        \n        // 2. Visit Root MIDDLE\n        System.out.print(node.val + " ");\n        \n        // 3. Traverse Right LAST\n        inOrder(node.right);\n    }\n}\n',"C++",'#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\n\nstruct Node {\n    int val;\n    Node* left;\n    Node* right;\n    Node(int x) : val(x), left(NULL), right(NULL) {}\n};\n\nvoid inOrder(Node* node) {\n    if (node == NULL) return;\n    \n    // 1. Traverse Left FIRST\n    inOrder(node->left);\n    \n    // 2. Visit Root MIDDLE\n    cout << node->val << " ";\n    \n    // 3. Traverse Right LAST\n    inOrder(node->right);\n}\n',"JavaScript","class Node {\n    constructor(val) {\n        this.val = val;\n        this.left = null;\n        this.right = null;\n    }\n}\n\nfunction inOrder(node) {\n    if (node === null) return;\n    \n    // 1. Traverse Left FIRST\n    inOrder(node.left);\n    \n    // 2. Visit Root MIDDLE\n    console.log(node.val);\n    \n    // 3. Traverse Right LAST\n    inOrder(node.right);\n}\n"],a0,a0),p,q,"In-order Traversal is a Depth-First Search (DFS) algorithm that processes the tree symmetrically. The golden rule is: Left \u2794 Root \u2794 Right. It plunges down the left side of the tree, processes the node, and then checks the right. This specific order gives it a superpower: when executed on a Binary Search Tree, it retrieves every single piece of data in perfectly sorted, ascending order without needing an expensive sorting algorithm!","tree-in-order-traversal",m,k,A.a(["Assuming it Sorts ANY Tree: In-order traversal only yields sorted data if the underlying tree is a valid Binary Search Tree (BST). Running it on a standard Binary Tree or a Heap just outputs a seemingly random order.","Missing the Base Case: Omitting the 'if (node == null) return;' check will immediately crash your traversal when it hits the bottom of the tree.","Modifying While Traversing: Attempting to insert or delete nodes in the middle of an In-order traversal is extremely dangerous and will severely corrupt the tree structure."],d),a,A.a([A.Z(1,"The prefix 'In' means the Root is processed IN the middle. Therefore, the sequence is Left, then Root, then Right.",A.a([g,f,h,e],d),"What is the exact processing sequence for an In-order Traversal?"),A.Z(2,"Because a BST stores smaller values to the left and larger to the right, visiting Left \u2794 Root \u2794 Right naturally processes the values from smallest to largest.",A.a(["It safely deletes all nodes from memory.","It creates an exact structural clone of the tree.","It retrieves all values from a Binary Search Tree in sorted (ascending) order.","It evaluates mathematical expressions in Postfix notation."],d),"What is the most famous property of an In-order traversal?"),A.Z(2,"A Min-Heap only guarantees that parents are smaller than children. It does not enforce that left children are smaller than right children. Therefore, In-order traversal on a Heap will NOT output sorted data.",A.a(["Yes, Heaps are a type of sorted tree.","Yes, In-order traversal sorts all binary trees.","No, Heaps only guarantee the root is the minimum, not a strict left-right ordering.","No, Heaps require Pre-order traversal to be sorted."],d),"If you perform an In-order traversal on a Min-Heap, will the output be sorted?"),A.Z(1,"By simply swapping the recursive calls (visiting the Right side first, then the Root, then the Left side), you process the largest values first, printing the tree in reverse sorted order!",A.a(["You can't. You must store the result in an array and reverse it.","Change the order to Right Subtree \u2794 Root \u2794 Left Subtree.","Use Post-order traversal instead.","Use Level-order traversal instead."],d),"How would you modify an In-order traversal to print a Binary Search Tree in DESCENDING (largest to smallest) order?")],a1),"assets/mds/dsa/trees/in_order.md",B.lV)
q=A.a(["Safe Deletion: Because it processes the children before the parent, Post-order is the only safe way to delete an entire tree from memory without leaving dangling pointers.","Postfix Evaluation: When applied to an Expression Tree, it outputs Reverse Polish Notation (Postfix), which is how computers naturally evaluate mathematical equations using a Stack.","Bottom-Up Calculations: Perfect for scenarios where a parent's value depends on its children (e.g., calculating the total file size of a directory tree or finding the height of a node)."],d)
p=A.a(["Useless for Sorting: Like Pre-order, Post-order does not yield sorted data when run on a Binary Search Tree.","Terrible for Cloning: If you try to build a tree by inserting nodes in Post-order, the tree structure will be completely corrupted because the root is inserted last.","Recursion Overhead: Being a Depth-First Search (DFS) algorithm, it relies heavily on the Call Stack. Very deep trees can cause a Stack Overflow."],d)
o=A.a(["Time Complexity: O(n) \u2014 Every node is visited exactly once.","Space Complexity (Balanced): O(log n) \u2014 The maximum depth of the recursive call stack is equal to the tree's height.","Space Complexity (Worst Case): O(n) \u2014 If the tree is highly unbalanced (like a Linked List), the call stack holds all 'n' nodes."],d)
q=A.c5(A.a5(["Python",'class Node:\n    def __init__(self, val):\n        self.val = val\n        self.left = None\n        self.right = None\n\ndef post_order(node):\n    if node is None:\n        return\n        \n    # 1. Traverse Left FIRST\n    post_order(node.left)\n    \n    # 2. Traverse Right SECOND\n    post_order(node.right)\n    \n    # 3. Visit Root LAST\n    print(node.val, end=" ")\n',"Java",'class Node {\n    int val;\n    Node left, right;\n    public Node(int item) { val = item; }\n}\n\nclass TreeTraversal {\n    public void postOrder(Node node) {\n        if (node == null) return;\n        \n        // 1. Traverse Left FIRST\n        postOrder(node.left);\n        \n        // 2. Traverse Right SECOND\n        postOrder(node.right);\n        \n        // 3. Visit Root LAST\n        System.out.print(node.val + " ");\n    }\n}\n',"C++",'#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\n\nstruct Node {\n    int val;\n    Node* left;\n    Node* right;\n    Node(int x) : val(x), left(NULL), right(NULL) {}\n};\n\nvoid postOrder(Node* node) {\n    if (node == NULL) return;\n    \n    // 1. Traverse Left FIRST\n    postOrder(node->left);\n    \n    // 2. Traverse Right SECOND\n    postOrder(node->right);\n    \n    // 3. Visit Root LAST\n    cout << node->val << " ";\n}\n',"JavaScript","class Node {\n    constructor(val) {\n        this.val = val;\n        this.left = null;\n        this.right = null;\n    }\n}\n\nfunction postOrder(node) {\n    if (node === null) return;\n    \n    // 1. Traverse Left FIRST\n    postOrder(node.left);\n    \n    // 2. Traverse Right SECOND\n    postOrder(node.right);\n    \n    // 3. Visit Root LAST\n    console.log(node.val);\n}\n"],a0,a0),o,p,"Post-order Traversal is the ultimate 'Bottom-Up' Depth-First Search (DFS) algorithm. The golden rule is: Left \u2794 Right \u2794 Root. It plunges all the way down to the deepest leaves, processes them, and then works its way backward up to the Root. This makes it the undisputed champion algorithm for safely deleting a tree from memory, as you can never delete a parent until you have successfully deleted all of its children.","tree-post-order-traversal",m,j,A.a(["Iterative Implementation Complexity: Writing Post-order iteratively (without recursion) is notoriously difficult compared to Pre-order and In-order, often requiring two Stacks or complex pointer tracking.","Misunderstanding the Root's Role: Thinking 'processing last' means the algorithm doesn't 'see' the root until the end. The algorithm physically passes through the root to get to the children, but delays *executing its logic* until the way back up.","Accidental Un-linking: Deleting a node's children but forgetting to set the parent's `left` or `right` pointers to `null`, causing the parent to point to garbage memory."],d),q,A.a([A.Z(3,"The prefix 'Post' means 'After'. Therefore, the Root is processed AFTER traversing both its Left and Right children.",A.a([g,f,e,h],d),"What is the exact processing sequence for a Post-order Traversal?"),A.Z(1,"If you delete a parent first, you lose the pointers to its children forever, causing a memory leak. Post-order guarantees children are deleted before their parent.",A.a(["Cloning a tree structure exactly.","Deleting an entire tree from memory in languages like C++.","Printing the values of a BST in ascending order.","Finding the shortest path between a node and the root."],d),"Which real-world scenario is Post-order traversal strictly required for?"),A.Z(2,"Post-order outputs Reverse Polish Notation (Postfix). Compilers love Postfix because it completely eliminates the need for parentheses when evaluating equations.",A.a(["Infix Notation (A + B * C)","Prefix Notation (* + A B C)","Postfix Notation (A B + C *)","Binary Notation"],d),"When you apply Post-order traversal to a Math Expression Tree (like `(A+B)*C`), what is the resulting output format?"),A.Z(3,"Because the algorithm dictates Left \u2794 Right \u2794 Root at every level, the main Root of the entire tree will wait for every single other node to be processed before it finally processes itself.",A.a(["The leftmost leaf node.","The rightmost leaf node.","The deepest node in the tree.","The main Root of the entire tree."],d),"In a Post-order traversal of a tree, which node is ALWAYS processed absolutely last?")],a1),"assets/mds/dsa/trees/post_order.md",B.lV)
p=A.a(["Foundation of Advanced Structures: Standard Binary Trees are the building blocks for Heaps, Binary Search Trees, Tries, and Huffman Coding trees.","Expression Parsing: Compilers use Binary Trees (Syntax Trees) to parse and evaluate mathematical and logical expressions.","Flexible Hierarchy: Unlike arrays or linked lists, binary trees provide a non-linear way to store data with exactly two potential branching paths per node."],d)
o=A.a(["No Implicit Sorting: Unlike a Binary Search Tree, a standard Binary Tree does not enforce an ordering rule. Searching for a specific value still takes O(n) time.","Pointer Overhead: Every single node requires memory for the data plus two additional pointers (left and right), which can waste memory if many nodes have single children.","Complex Deletion: Removing a node from the middle of a standard binary tree while keeping the rest of the tree intact is algorithmically messy."],d)
